Environmental Simulation for Laboratory Experiments
Building on our previous study,1 where we introduced an NDIR-based CH4 sensor featuring
a MEMS-pyroelectric detector and a mid-IR source, the current objective is to characterize
the environmental impact factors affecting the sensor’s performance. The NDIR instrument
used in this study consists of both commercial and in-house components. Specifically, the
CH4 detection system utilizes a commercially available IR detector, IR source, and com
patible data acquisition module and software. The customization includes designing sensor
cells of various dimensions and assembling the components in-house to meet experimental
requirements. In this improved setup, after the gas reaches the target humidity, it is mixed
with pure CH4 to achieve varying concentrations. Given the very small amount of CH4
injected, the impact on relative humidity is considered negligible. Once the concentration of
the mixed gas stabilizes, it is injected into the gas cell at a constant flow rate of 500 mL/min
for 1 min, after which the solenoid valves controlling the inlet and outlet are automatically
closed to perform signal measurement.
Absorption spectrometry measurements were conducted using a custom-made aluminum
alloy gas cell (length ≈ 30 cm, volume ≈ 46.2 mL), fitted with 3D-printed saddles and caps,
and ultra-transparent quartz sheets sealed with silicone-based adhesive to ensure airtightness.
The gas cell featured solenoid valves and a pressure gauge to monitor the pressure before the
gas enters the cell, ensuring stable airflow. Data acquisition was achieved with a Fabry-P ́erot
Interferometer connection board, integrated with a LiTaO3-based pyroelectric detector and
Fabry-P ́erot filters (LFP-3144C-337 model from InfraTec, 3100 ∼ 4400 nm), and the IR
source (EMIRS200 from Axetris), which emitted wavelengths from 2 to 14 μm. The system
was connected to a PC via USB, housed in an insulated box for temperature stability, with
thermocouples monitoring both the gas cell and ambient temperatures. To identify the
optimal CH4 detection wavelength, we first conducted a broad scan (10 nm step size) across
the 3100 ∼ 4400 nm range, identifying a response window between 3200 ∼ 3500 nm. A finer
2 nm scan revealed 3346 nm as the optimal wavelength, confirmed through experimental and
