Field Testing at the INAS
We conducted the measurements using the experimental setup illustrated in Figure S3, which
was designed to monitor surface soil gas emissions with a focus on the simultaneous mea
surement of CO2 and CH4 using a chamber-based method.2–4 The setup features a closed
chamber placed on the soil surface to trap gases emitted from the soil, preventing gas ex
change with the atmosphere, while external air, serving as a reference, is directed toward
the sensors. The system includes two NDIR-based sensor arrays: the upper sensor is the LI
840 CO2/H2O gas analyzer, a fully commercial product from LI-COR, specifically designed
for precise CO2 concentration measurements. The lower sensor, as described earlier in this
study, is a custom-designed NDIR-based CH4 sensor featuring a LiTaO3-based pyroelectric
detector with Fabry-Pe ́rot filters and an EMIRS200 IR emitter.
The measurement system utilized an STM32 microcontroller and a series of relays to
control solenoid valves, alternating between the reference gas (atmospheric air) and the
target gas (soil gas). Atmospheric air was used as the reference for comparative analysis,
based on the assumption that short-term fluctuations in atmospheric CH4 levels (e.g., within
24 h) were negligible due to the good air circulation in the field. A vacuum pump, labeled
“M,” maintained a constant airflow through the sensors at a rate of 500 mL/min, ensuring
stable gas delivery during the measurements.
Sequential measurements were conducted for both gas types, with each analysis lasting 2
min, followed by a 5 min rest period. The field test spanned a 21-h period, capturing varia
tions in CO2 and CH4 levels under different temperature conditions during both daytime and
nighttime. To provide further insight into CH4 measurements, relevant soil characteristics
were also tracked.
Prior to field deployment, the NDIR sensors were calibrated in the laboratory at approx
imately 25 °C to ensure measurement accuracy. For the NDIR-based CO2 sensor, dry N2 gas
was used as the zero reference, while the NDIR-based CH4 sensor was calibrated using both
dry N2 gas and a 1000 ppm standard CH4 gas mixture. During the field test, recalibrations
