Cross-Influence of Temperature and Humidity
These findings highlight the significant impact of temperature and humidity on CH4 detection
performance, underscoring the importance of carefully controlled environmental conditions
to achieve optimal results. As shown in Figure S5, high-temperature and low-humidity
environments demonstrate more favorable detection limits compared to low-temperature
and high-humidity conditions. This advantage arises from reduced water vapor interference
and enhanced signal resolution, which mitigate the overlap between H2O and CH4 absorption
bands.
Figure S5: Detection limits under different temperature and humidity conditions.
In contrast, low-temperature and high-humidity conditions amplify the influence of wa
ter molecules on detection. The overlap of the infrared absorption bands of H2O and CH4
becomes more pronounced, especially as temperature-induced state changes, such as con
densation near the dew point, further complicate measurements. These challenges are par
