Field Test Insights on CH4 Baseline Dynamics
Soil physico-chemical sensors were deployed to measure the physical and chemical properties
of the soil to investigate its basic characteristics.11–13 The statistics for basic soil property
indicators summarized in Table S5 indicate that the mean soil temperature T was approxi
mately 27.9 °C with a standard deviation of 8.9 °C. Soil relative humidity RH had a mean of
23.5% and exhibited low variation. Soil salinity S averaged 193.6 ppm, with values ranging
from 143 ppm to 236 ppm. The soil pH averaged 6.4, indicating slightly acidic conditions,
while minimal variation was observed in conductivity C consistently measured at 200 μS/cm.
Similarly, we measured nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium levels, which were consistently
at 15 mg/kg, 21 mg/kg, and 53 mg/kg, respectively, although these are not shown in the
table. This suggests a stable chemical environment for soil nutrients, with more variability
observed in physical properties such as temperature and salinity.
As described above, the field test was conducted in a relatively stable soil environment,
where no significant CH4 emissions were anticipated. Despite this, a notable lag in the CO2
signal was observed, likely due to delayed biological responses, including plant respiration
and soil microbial activity. The CH4 and CO2 signals were measured using two separate
NDIR devices, each tuned to its respective wavelength. Interestingly, the CH4 signal trend
closely followed that of the CO2 signal, showing a strong correlation. This suggests that
fluctuations in CO2 concentrations significantly influenced the CH4 response, reinforcing
findings from our laboratory CO2 selectivity tests. Our background survey, which included
information on local vegetation, fauna, and industrial activities, further confirmed that the
absence of substantial biological activity in the area (e.g., wetlands or permafrost) accounted
for the lack of CH4 emissions. These results highlight that, even in a stable environment,
CH4 detection can still be impacted by CO2 fluctuations, underscoring the need to account
for cross-interference in real-world sensor applications.
