Bias Correction Accuracy of Machine Learning Models
Figure S7 shows the performance of various models on both the training and cross-validation
sets. The learning curves, which plot model performance against the size of the train
ing dataset, provide insight into how the model’s accuracy evolves as more data is intro
duced.14–17 In this case, linear regression demonstrates poor performance, with both the
training and cross-validation scores being consistently low and showing high variability. This
indicates that linear regression struggles with generalization and does not perform well re
gardless of the dataset size.
Decision tree, random forest, and gradient boosting also show variability in their per
formance with small datasets. When the sample size is below 100, their training scores are
relatively high, but the cross-validation scores exhibit a large standard deviation, indicating
the presence of overfitting and instability due to the limited number of samples. However, as
the dataset size increases, their performance improves, and the overfitting issues diminish,
with gradient boosting showing better generalization compared to decision tree and random
forest.
